Security: Tokens and passwords in the request address are masked in the log

Every entry in owncloud.log and in syslog records the address of the request
that wrote it, including the query string. WOPI clients such as Collabora
Online append the access token of the opened document as access_token to
every request, so each entry written during such a request contained a valid
token in plain text. The same applied to requests that carried requesttoken
or password in their query string. The logged address now shows *** instead
of the values of the query parameters access_token, requesttoken and
password, for the log types owncloud and syslog. The office app oco_office
relies on this and does not open documents on a server without it.

Only these three query parameters are masked. Tokens in the path of an
address, for example in the links to reset a password or to confirm a mail
address, other query parameters such as log_secret, and the text of messages
and exceptions are logged as before. Log files written before the update and
the access logs of the web server and of Collabora Online may still contain
such tokens; rotate or clean them up.
